/* V92 — THE BLOOM gets somewhere to happen.

   V87's header wrote down the risk and then took it: "the palettes and skylines stay shared, because
   a map is a cast and a sequence rather than a whole new renderer." That was the right call to ship
   the machinery on, and it is the wrong thing to leave standing. As it is, a player who beats fifteen
   stages and unlocks a second map walks into a place called SEEDBED and finds map one's nebula over
   map one's city skyline, with new monsters in front of it. Six new creatures cannot carry a world on
   their own — the screen is nine parts background — and the exact sentence V87 was worried about
   ("the kind of detail that quietly tells a player the second map is a reskin") is what the second
   map currently says out loud.

   ---- Nothing here is a new renderer ----

   V26 already parameterised all of this and it is worth saying how well, because it is why this part
   is data and six painters rather than a rewrite. `V26_SCENERY` is a table of six, one per world,
   naming a sky body, a skyline, and the four colours that decide the mood: `sun`, `haze`, `horizon`
   and an optional `mid`. `V26_SKYLINES` is a plain object of named painter functions, so a new
   silhouette is a new key. `v26SkyBody` switches on a kind. The colours the creatures are lit by come
   from `worlds[i]`.

   So a second map's world is: six scenery rows, six painters, two sky bodies, six palettes, and a
   swap on install. No drawing code in the arena changes, and map one is byte-for-byte what it was —
   its rows are snapshotted at load and put back when you travel home.

   ---- The one trap, and it is a real one ----

   V26 caches every pre-rendered layer and every full-screen gradient, keyed by **world index**:
   `sky-line|0|1|390x148|3` and `sky|0|844`. Index 0 is SEEDBED on one map and NEBULA FORGE on the
   other. Swapping the table without clearing those caches leaves the Legion's foundry towers standing
   on the Bloom's ground, painted once and then never questioned again, because a cache hit does not
   re-read the table it was built from. Both maps are cleared on every install.

   ---- Six places, and why they are six different shapes ----

   The temptation with a forest is six kinds of tree, which is one shape six times. Each of these is
   built to have a different silhouette at a glance, because the skyline is what a player reads
   peripherally while looking at the middle of the screen:

     SEEDBED       low mounds and thin sprouts. Ground-hugging, mostly sky — deliberately the
                   emptiest horizon in the game, so arriving here reads as an opening.
     THE THICKET   crossing thorn arches. No verticals at all; the shape is tangle.
     ASH ROWS      burnt stumps on a regular pitch. The only skyline with a rhythm, because the
                   name is the mechanic: something planted these in rows and then it burned.
     HEARTWOOD     three trunks across the whole width, cut faces showing rings. Scale contrast —
                   after four worlds of small things, one thing that does not fit on screen.
     DEEP LOAM     inverted: roots hanging from above, caps below. The only ceiling in the game.
     THE CANOPY    overlapping leaf lobes filling from below with light between them. The only
                   skyline you are looking up through rather than at.

   Three new sky bodies and two of map one's reused, which is not the split this part started with.
   The plan was two new and three reused, on the assumption that `v26SkyBody` keying its cache on
   `w.accent` and `w.enemy` meant a reused body would repaint in the new world's colours. Photographed,
   it does not: `eclipse` and `sun` read their colours from `scene`, but `giant` hardcodes its own
   teal — `lit(R,'#0d3a44','#4fd6c0')` and five teal storm bands — so HEARTWOOD, a world lit amber
   from end to end, had a blue-green gas giant hanging in it. A cache key that includes a colour is
   not the same as a painter that uses it. HEARTWOOD got its own body instead, and it is a better one
   than the reuse would have been: a cut face with growth rings in the sky, answering the rings on
   the trunks below. */

/* ---- The palettes -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `enemy` is not just the nebula's second colour: V81 pulls every creature's hue halfway towards
   `worlds[world].enemy`, so this table is also what the cast is lit by. The Bloom's greens are
   therefore given to the GROUND and the golds and corals to `enemy`, not the other way round —
   creatures the colour of the world they stand in is the exact failure V81 was written to fix, and
   the monochrome test at the end of this pass is what confirms it did not come back. */

const V92_BLOOM_WORLDS=[
 {bg:['#04100b','#123d24'],accent:'#8dff9e',enemy:'#ffd45e',boss:'THE FIRST SEED'},
 {bg:['#06090f','#1d3014'],accent:'#b6ff5e',enemy:'#ff7a3d',boss:'THE THORN CROWN'},
 {bg:['#0d0806','#3a2114'],accent:'#ffb46b',enemy:'#ff5a4d',boss:'THE LAST STANDING'},
 {bg:['#0a0603','#46280c'],accent:'#ffc861',enemy:'#ff6fae',boss:'HEARTWOOD ITSELF'},
 {bg:['#04030a','#241040'],accent:'#b98cff',enemy:'#55f0c8',boss:'THE DEEP MYCELIUM'},
 {bg:['#030c07','#0f4a2c'],accent:'#7dffcf',enemy:'#ffe066',boss:'WHAT THE CANOPY HIDES'}
];

const V92_BLOOM_SCENERY=[
 {sky:'bloomhead',skyline:'seedbed',  sun:'#d8ffb0',haze:'#4fb36a',horizon:'#0d2a18',sx:.76,sy:.23,ss:.60},
 {sky:'veiled',   skyline:'bramble',  sun:'#e8ffc0',haze:'#4f8f2c',horizon:'#101f0c',sx:.26,sy:.20,ss:.56},
 /* The burnt world keeps map one's sun because a sun is what burned it, and gets its own `mid` for
    the same reason SOLAR TEMPLE needed one: an orange sky that meets an orange ground with nothing
    between them loses the horizon, and the horizon is where the skyline has to read. */
 {sky:'sun',      skyline:'ashrow',   sun:'#ffd0a0',haze:'#c46a35',horizon:'#2e150a',sx:.72,sy:.25,ss:.68,mid:'#2b1a12'},
 {sky:'ringed',   skyline:'heartwood',sun:'#ffe3a8',haze:'#b8792c',horizon:'#3a1f07',sx:.78,sy:.21,ss:.80},
 {sky:'eclipse',  skyline:'loam',     sun:'#ddc6ff',haze:'#7b4bd0',horizon:'#180a30',sx:.5, sy:.24,ss:.62},
 {sky:'veiled',   skyline:'canopy',   sun:'#eaffd0',haze:'#35a86e',horizon:'#0a3320',sx:.22,sy:.19,ss:.66}
];

/* Snapshotted rather than reconstructed, so map one is exactly what it was rather than what this
   part believes it was. Taken at load, which is after V87's install has run and rewritten the world
   NAMES — names are V87's to own and are deliberately not copied here. */
const V92_HOME_WORLDS=worlds.map((w)=>({bg:w.bg.slice(),accent:w.accent,enemy:w.enemy,boss:w.boss}));
const V92_HOME_SCENERY=V26_SCENERY.map((s)=>({...s}));

function v92Apply(mapIndex){
 const bloom=mapIndex===1;
 const pal=bloom?V92_BLOOM_WORLDS:V92_HOME_WORLDS;
 const scn=bloom?V92_BLOOM_SCENERY:V92_HOME_SCENERY;
 for(let i=0;i<worlds.length;i++){
  const p=pal[i%pal.length];
  worlds[i].bg=p.bg.slice();
  worlds[i].accent=p.accent;
  worlds[i].enemy=p.enemy;
  worlds[i].boss=p.boss;
  /* Replaced rather than assigned over: `mid` is present on some rows and absent on others, and
     Object.assign would leave a stale `mid` from the row underneath. Nothing captures a scenery row
     across frames — `drawBackground` reads `V26_SCENERY[index]` every frame — so replacing the
     element is safe and copying it is not enough. */
  V26_SCENERY[i]={...scn[i%scn.length]};
 }
 /* See the header: these are keyed by world INDEX, which means the same key names two different
    places once a second map exists. */
 if(typeof v26Layers!=='undefined')v26Layers.clear();
 if(typeof v26Fills!=='undefined')v26Fills.clear();
 /* V81 caches the converged creature colours per world, and every one of them was converged towards
    an `enemy` that has just changed. */
 if(typeof v81SkinCache!=='undefined'){v81SkinCache=null;v81SkinWorld=-1;}
}

/* ---- The sky bodies -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Wrapped rather than edited into V26's own switch, which keeps the two new ones and the reasoning
   for them in the part that needs them. Falls through for every kind it does not own, so map one's
   five bodies are reached by exactly the code that always reached them. */

const V92_BODIES=['bloomhead','veiled','ringed'];
const v92SkyBody=v26SkyBody;
v26SkyBody=function(kind,size,w,scene){
 if(V92_BODIES.indexOf(kind)<0)return v92SkyBody(kind,size,w,scene);
 return v26Layer(`sky|${kind}|${Math.round(size)}|${w.accent}|${w.enemy}|${DPR}`,size,size,(c,S)=>{
  const cx=S/2,cy=S*.5,R=S*.3;
  const glow=c.createRadialGradient(cx,cy,R*.85,cx,cy,S*.5);
  glow.addColorStop(0,scene.haze+'4a');
  glow.addColorStop(.5,scene.haze+'14');
  glow.addColorStop(1,scene.haze+'00');
  c.fillStyle=glow;c.fillRect(0,0,S,S);

  if(kind==='bloomhead'){
   /* A seed head hanging into frame on a stalk that leaves the top of the screen. The stalk is what
      makes it an object in the world rather than a light in the sky, and it is the reason this body
      sits high and to one side in every scenery row that uses it: a stalk that starts below the top
      edge reads as a balloon. */
   /* Photographed first as a single dark stroke and it was not there: `#0a2416` against a sky whose
      own colour is `#04100b` is one shade of near-black on another, so the head read as a lollipop
      floating with nothing holding it. Drawn twice — the dark body, then a lit edge down one side
      from the same direction the head is lit from. A silhouette needs something behind it or
      something on it, and a stalk crossing an empty sky has neither. */
   const stalk=(width,style)=>{
    c.strokeStyle=style;
    c.lineWidth=Math.max(1,S*width);
    c.lineCap='round';
    c.beginPath();
    c.moveTo(cx+S*.06,-2);
    c.quadraticCurveTo(cx+S*.10,cy-R*1.5,cx,cy-R*.86);
    c.stroke();
   };
   stalk(.034,'#0a2416');
   c.save();
   c.globalCompositeOperation='lighter';
   c.translate(-S*.009,0);
   stalk(.013,scene.sun+'55');
   c.restore();

   /* The head: lit from the upper left like every other body in this table, so the whole game agrees
      about where the light is. */
   const g=c.createRadialGradient(cx-R*.45,cy-R*.5,R*.05,cx,cy,R);
   g.addColorStop(0,'#ffffff');
   g.addColorStop(.18,scene.sun);
   g.addColorStop(.62,w.accent+'aa');
   g.addColorStop(1,'#05130c');
   c.fillStyle=g;
   c.beginPath();c.arc(cx,cy,R,0,Math.PI*2);c.fill();

   /* Seams. Without them this is a green sphere, and a green sphere in the sky of a space game is a
      planet however it is coloured — the whole point of putting a seed head up there is that it is
      not one. Five curves from crown to base, spaced by their cosine so they crowd towards the limb
      the way segments on anything round actually do, clipped to the body. */
   c.save();
   c.beginPath();c.arc(cx,cy,R,0,Math.PI*2);c.clip();
   for(let i=0;i<5;i++){
    const k=-1+ (i+.5)/5*2;
    const off=Math.sin(k*Math.PI*.5)*R;
    c.strokeStyle='#04120a55';
    c.lineWidth=Math.max(1,R*.045);
    c.beginPath();
    c.moveTo(cx+off*.12,cy-R*1.02);
    c.quadraticCurveTo(cx+off,cy,cx+off*.12,cy+R*1.02);
    c.stroke();
   }
   c.restore();

   /* Filaments drooping off the underside, each ending in a lit tip. Drawn dark first and lit at the
      tip only: a fully additive fringe turns the body into a starburst and loses the sphere. */
   const rnd=v26Rand(41);
   for(let i=0;i<11;i++){
    const a=Math.PI*.12+ (i/10)*Math.PI*.76;
    const ox=cx+Math.cos(a)*R*.92,oy=cy+Math.sin(a)*R*.92;
    const len=R*(.34+rnd()*.5);
    const sway=(rnd()-.5)*R*.4;
    c.strokeStyle='#0b2a19';
    c.lineWidth=Math.max(1,R*.035);
    c.beginPath();
    c.moveTo(ox,oy);
    c.quadraticCurveTo(ox+sway,oy+len*.6,ox+sway*1.4,oy+len);
    c.stroke();
    c.save();
    c.globalCompositeOperation='lighter';
    c.fillStyle=w.enemy+'cc';
    c.beginPath();c.arc(ox+sway*1.4,oy+len,Math.max(1.2,R*.045),0,Math.PI*2);c.fill();
    c.restore();
   }
   /* Spores leaving it. Three, not thirty — this is a landmark, not weather. */
   c.save();
   c.globalCompositeOperation='lighter';
   c.fillStyle=scene.sun+'66';
   for(let i=0;i<3;i++){
    c.beginPath();
    c.arc(cx+(rnd()-.5)*S*.5,cy+R*(1.2+rnd()*.8),Math.max(1,R*.03),0,Math.PI*2);
    c.fill();
   }
   c.restore();
   return;
  }

  if(kind==='ringed'){
   /* HEARTWOOD's sky: a cut face. Growth rings on a disc, off-centre, with one split running from
      the heart to the edge — the same drawing the trunks on the ground carry, at the scale of a
      moon. The point is that the world's landmark and its architecture are the same object seen
      twice, which is a thing a place does and a backdrop does not. */
   const g=c.createRadialGradient(cx-R*.4,cy-R*.45,R*.05,cx,cy,R);
   g.addColorStop(0,scene.sun);
   g.addColorStop(.35,'#c98a3e');
   g.addColorStop(.78,'#6b3f16');
   g.addColorStop(1,'#1a0d04');
   c.fillStyle=g;
   c.beginPath();c.arc(cx,cy,R,0,Math.PI*2);c.fill();

   c.save();
   c.beginPath();c.arc(cx,cy,R,0,Math.PI*2);c.clip();
   /* The heart sits off centre, because rings are only legible as rings when they are not
      concentric with the outline that contains them. */
   const hx=cx-R*.22,hy=cy+R*.16;
   const rnd=v26Rand(53);
   for(let i=1;i<=11;i++){
    const rr=R*(i/11)*1.35;
    c.strokeStyle=(i%3===0?scene.sun+'44':'#00000038');
    c.lineWidth=Math.max(.8,R*(i%3===0?.012:.022));
    c.beginPath();
    c.ellipse(hx,hy,rr,rr*(.9+rnd()*.16),.2,0,Math.PI*2);
    c.stroke();
   }
   /* The split. One, from the heart outward, widening as it goes. */
   c.fillStyle='#120802cc';
   c.beginPath();
   c.moveTo(hx,hy);
   c.lineTo(cx+R*1.4,cy-R*.75);
   c.lineTo(cx+R*1.4,cy-R*.42);
   c.closePath();c.fill();
   c.restore();

   /* Sap at the heart, the one warm light, matching the sap on the trunks. */
   c.save();
   c.globalCompositeOperation='lighter';
   const sap=c.createRadialGradient(cx-R*.22,cy+R*.16,0,cx-R*.22,cy+R*.16,R*.3);
   sap.addColorStop(0,w.enemy+'66');
   sap.addColorStop(1,'transparent');
   c.fillStyle=sap;
   c.beginPath();c.arc(cx-R*.22,cy+R*.16,R*.3,0,Math.PI*2);c.fill();
   c.restore();
   return;
  }

  /* 'veiled' — a pale moon with leaves crossing in front of it. The leaves are the point: they are
     the only thing in the sky that says the player is UNDER something, which is what separates a
     forest from a field, and they cost four filled curves. */
  const g=c.createRadialGradient(cx-R*.42,cy-R*.5,R*.05,cx,cy,R);
  g.addColorStop(0,'#ffffff');
  g.addColorStop(.22,scene.sun);
  g.addColorStop(.72,scene.haze+'88');
  g.addColorStop(1,'#040a06');
  c.fillStyle=g;
  c.beginPath();c.arc(cx,cy,R,0,Math.PI*2);c.fill();

  /* Craters before the leaves, clipped to the disc, so the leaves sit on top of a body rather than
     on top of a flat circle. */
  c.save();
  c.beginPath();c.arc(cx,cy,R,0,Math.PI*2);c.clip();
  const crnd=v26Rand(23);
  c.fillStyle='#00000022';
  for(let i=0;i<6;i++){
   c.beginPath();
   c.arc(cx+(crnd()-.5)*R*1.5,cy+(crnd()-.5)*R*1.5,R*(.06+crnd()*.13),0,Math.PI*2);
   c.fill();
  }
  c.restore();

  /* Photographed at len up to 1.9R and width .12R and they read as blades slashing across the sky —
     a thin dark sliver longer than the thing it crosses is a scratch, not a leaf. Shorter, fatter,
     more of them, and each with a midrib, which is the one mark that makes a lens shape read as
     foliage at this size. They still overhang the edge, deliberately: a leaf entirely inside the
     disc reads as a marking ON the moon rather than as something in front of it. */
  const lrnd=v26Rand(29);
  for(let i=0;i<8;i++){
   const a=-1.15+i*.3+(lrnd()-.5)*.35;
   const len=R*(.72+lrnd()*.55);
   const wide=R*(.2+lrnd()*.16);
   const ox=cx+(lrnd()-.5)*R*1.6;
   const oy=cy+(lrnd()-.5)*R*1.6;
   c.save();
   c.translate(ox,oy);
   c.rotate(a);
   c.fillStyle='#030b07dd';
   c.beginPath();
   c.moveTo(-len*.5,0);
   c.quadraticCurveTo(0,-wide,len*.5,0);
   c.quadraticCurveTo(0,wide,-len*.5,0);
   c.closePath();c.fill();
   /* A lit edge on the side facing the light, or the leaves read as holes punched in the moon. */
   c.strokeStyle=scene.sun+'3a';
   c.lineWidth=Math.max(.8,R*.016);
   c.beginPath();
   c.moveTo(-len*.5,0);
   c.quadraticCurveTo(0,-wide,len*.5,0);
   c.stroke();
   /* The midrib, stopping short of the tip the way a real one does. */
   c.strokeStyle=scene.sun+'26';
   c.lineWidth=Math.max(.6,R*.01);
   c.beginPath();
   c.moveTo(-len*.44,0);
   c.lineTo(len*.4,0);
   c.stroke();
   c.restore();
  }
 });
};

/* ---- The skylines -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Assigned onto V26's table rather than wrapping anything: `V26_SKYLINES` is a plain object looked up
   by name at paint time, which is the seam it was written with. Each of these is called once per
   size per world and cached, so they can afford to be more expensive than a per-frame path — and all
   of them are, deliberately: this is the one place in the game where detail is free.

   Every painter takes (c,w,h,pal,rnd,near). `rnd` is V26's seeded generator, so a repaint after a
   rotation produces the skyline the player was just looking at rather than a new one. `near` is the
   front range, which is bigger, darker and scrolls faster. */

/* A leaf: pointed where it joins the stem, blunt at the far end, fattest past the middle.

   Written first as a symmetric lens — a point at BOTH ends — and photographed hanging off the canopy
   it read as a cave full of stalactites. A shape that tapers to a point at the end furthest from
   what holds it is a spike; the asymmetry is the entire difference, and it costs one bezier over one
   quadratic. Width matters as much: past about three to one, any silhouette reads as a spine
   whatever its ends are doing, so callers pass fat. */
function v92Leaf(c,len,wide){
 c.beginPath();
 c.moveTo(0,0);
 c.bezierCurveTo(len*.28,-wide,len*.8,-wide*.86,len,-wide*.1);
 c.bezierCurveTo(len*.8,wide*.86,len*.28,wide,0,0);
 c.closePath();
 c.fill();
}

V26_SKYLINES.seedbed=function(c,w,h,pal,rnd,near){
 /* Mounds first, as one continuous ground line rather than separate humps: a row of discrete bumps
    reads as objects sitting on a floor, and this is meant to read as the floor itself. */
 c.fillStyle=near?'#03150d':'#072319';
 c.beginPath();
 c.moveTo(-10,h);
 let x=-10;
 while(x<w+10){
  const step=h*(near?.5:.36)*(.7+rnd()*.9);
  const rise=h*(near?.3:.2)*(.35+rnd()*.8);
  c.quadraticCurveTo(x+step*.5,h-rise,x+step,h-rise*.35);
  x+=step;
 }
 c.lineTo(w+10,h);
 c.closePath();
 c.fill();

 /* Sprouts. Thin, tall relative to the mounds, and topped with a pod that carries the world's
    colour — out here the pods are the only light, which is the same rule map one's garden used and
    the reason these two worlds read as related rather than identical. */
 x=-6;
 while(x<w+6){
  const th=h*(near?.62:.44)*(.35+rnd()*.9);
  const lean=(rnd()-.5)*h*.16;
  const base=h-h*(near?.16:.1)*rnd();
  c.strokeStyle=near?'#03150d':'#072319';
  c.lineWidth=Math.max(1,h*(near?.014:.009));
  c.beginPath();
  c.moveTo(x,base);
  c.quadraticCurveTo(x+lean*.4,base-th*.6,x+lean,base-th);
  c.stroke();
  c.save();
  c.globalCompositeOperation='lighter';
  c.fillStyle=pal.accent+(near?'66':'3a');
  c.beginPath();
  c.ellipse(x+lean,base-th,Math.max(1.2,h*(near?.02:.014)),Math.max(1.6,h*(near?.032:.022)),lean*.02,0,Math.PI*2);
  c.fill();
  c.restore();
  x+=h*(near?.14:.09)*(.5+rnd());
 }
};

V26_SKYLINES.bramble=function(c,w,h,pal,rnd,near){
 /* No trunks and no baseline: arches springing from the floor and landing back on it, crossing each
    other. Tangle is the silhouette, so the arches are drawn as thick strokes that overlap rather
    than as filled shapes that would merge into one mass. */
 const ink=near?'#050d04':'#0b1608';
 const arcs=[];
 let x=-h*.4;
 while(x<w+h*.4){
  const span=h*(near?1.0:.7)*(.6+rnd()*.9);
  const rise=h*(near?.86:.6)*(.45+rnd()*.7);
  arcs.push([x,span,rise]);
  x+=h*(near?.34:.24)*(.5+rnd());
 }
 c.lineCap='round';
 for(const [ax,span,rise] of arcs){
  c.strokeStyle=ink;
  c.lineWidth=Math.max(1.6,h*(near?.038:.024));
  c.beginPath();
  c.moveTo(ax,h+2);
  c.quadraticCurveTo(ax+span*.5,h-rise,ax+span,h+2);
  c.stroke();
  /* Thorns along the outside of each arch. Placed by walking the curve so they point away from it —
     spikes on a random tangent are the difference between a bramble and a scribble. */
  const n=near?7:5;
  for(let i=1;i<n;i++){
   const t=i/n;
   const px=(1-t)*(1-t)*ax+2*(1-t)*t*(ax+span*.5)+t*t*(ax+span);
   const py=(1-t)*(1-t)*(h+2)+2*(1-t)*t*(h-rise)+t*t*(h+2);
   const dx=2*(1-t)*(span*.5)+2*t*(span*.5);
   const dy=2*(1-t)*(-rise-2)+2*t*(rise+2);
   const m=Math.hypot(dx,dy)||1;
   const len=h*(near?.07:.045)*(.6+rnd()*.8);
   c.strokeStyle=ink;
   c.lineWidth=Math.max(1,h*(near?.014:.009));
   c.beginPath();
   c.moveTo(px,py);
   c.lineTo(px-dy/m*len,py+dx/m*len);
   c.stroke();
  }
  /* One bud per arch, at the crown, and only sometimes. Light in a thicket has to be scarce or the
     tangle stops reading as dense. */
  if(rnd()<.45){
   c.save();
   c.globalCompositeOperation='lighter';
   c.fillStyle=pal.accent+(near?'55':'30');
   c.beginPath();
   c.arc(ax+span*.5,h-rise*.5,Math.max(1.2,h*(near?.02:.013)),0,Math.PI*2);
   c.fill();
   c.restore();
  }
 }
};

V26_SKYLINES.ashrow=function(c,w,h,pal,rnd,near){
 /* The regular pitch is the whole idea and it is the only regular thing in six worlds, so the jitter
    on the spacing is deliberately tiny — enough that it is not a comb, not enough to lose the row. */
 const pitch=h*(near?.30:.21);
 let x=-pitch;
 while(x<w+pitch){
  const th=h*(near?.66:.46)*(.55+rnd()*.5);
  /* Widened from .05: at the first width these photographed as a picket fence of spikes rather than
     as stumps. A stump is short and thick relative to what it used to be, and the ratio is the only
     thing carrying that. */
  const tw=Math.max(3,h*(near?.085:.058)*(.75+rnd()*.5));
  const lean=(rnd()-.5)*h*.06;
  c.save();
  c.translate(x+(rnd()-.5)*pitch*.16,h);
  c.fillStyle=near?'#0b0705':'#171009';
  /* Snapped off rather than tapered to a point: these were cut or burned through, and a broken top
     is a diagonal with a splinter, not a cone. */
  c.beginPath();
  c.moveTo(-tw,0);
  c.lineTo(-tw*.7+lean,-th);
  c.lineTo(lean+tw*.1,-th*(.82+rnd()*.12));
  c.lineTo(tw*.7+lean,-th*.94);
  c.lineTo(tw,0);
  c.closePath();c.fill();
  /* Embers still in the stump. On the near range only: two ranges of glowing dots is a starfield at
     ground level. */
  if(near&&rnd()<.5){
   c.save();
   c.globalCompositeOperation='lighter';
   c.fillStyle=pal.enemy+'55';
   for(let i=0;i<2;i++){
    c.beginPath();
    c.arc(lean+(rnd()-.5)*tw,-th*(.3+rnd()*.5),Math.max(1,h*.012),0,Math.PI*2);
    c.fill();
   }
   c.restore();
  }
  c.restore();
  /* Smoke: one thin rising line off some of them, fading out. Vertical against a horizon of
     verticals, so it is kept faint and sparse and reads as air rather than as more stumps. */
  if(rnd()<.28){
   c.save();
   c.globalAlpha=near?.16:.1;
   c.strokeStyle=pal.accent;
   c.lineWidth=Math.max(1,h*.008);
   c.beginPath();
   c.moveTo(x,h-th*.9);
   c.quadraticCurveTo(x+h*.06,h-th*1.4,x-h*.03,h-th*1.9);
   c.stroke();
   c.restore();
  }
  x+=pitch;
 }
};

V26_SKYLINES.heartwood=function(c,w,h,pal,rnd,near){
 /* Three trunks across the entire width on the near range. After four worlds of things that fit on
    screen, one that does not is the cheapest scale cue there is, and the ring faces are what make
    the size legible — a shape with no detail on it has no size. */
 const count=near?3:5;
 const pitch=w/count;
 for(let i=0;i<=count;i++){
  const cxT=i*pitch+(rnd()-.5)*pitch*.3;
  const tw=pitch*(near?.42:.3)*(.7+rnd()*.5);
  c.fillStyle=near?'#100802':'#1c1206';
  c.beginPath();
  c.moveTo(cxT-tw,h);
  c.quadraticCurveTo(cxT-tw*.72,h*.4,cxT-tw*.56,-2);
  c.lineTo(cxT+tw*.56,-2);
  c.quadraticCurveTo(cxT+tw*.72,h*.4,cxT+tw,h);
  c.closePath();c.fill();
  /* Growth rings, drawn as nested arcs on the lit side of the trunk and clipped to it. Concentric
     ellipses centred on the trunk would read as a target; offset to one edge they read as a cut. */
  c.save();
  c.beginPath();
  c.moveTo(cxT-tw,h);
  c.quadraticCurveTo(cxT-tw*.72,h*.4,cxT-tw*.56,-2);
  c.lineTo(cxT+tw*.56,-2);
  c.quadraticCurveTo(cxT+tw*.72,h*.4,cxT+tw,h);
  c.closePath();
  c.clip();
  /* First version drew nested ellipses whose vertical radius grew as `h*.5*r*.5` — by the third
     ring that is taller than the strip, so five of the six were entirely off the sprite and the
     one that showed was a faint arc at 13% alpha. Photographed as flat dark columns.

     Concentric arcs about a heart just OUTSIDE the trunk's near edge, stepping by a fixed fraction
     of the trunk's own width, so every ring lands on the trunk and the curvature reads as a cut
     face turned slightly away rather than as a target painted on a post. */
  const hx=cxT-tw*1.05,hy=h*.55;
  for(let r=1;r<=9;r++){
   const rr=tw*.34*r;
   c.strokeStyle=r%3===0?pal.accent+(near?'3a':'22'):'#00000044';
   c.lineWidth=Math.max(1,h*(r%3===0?.006:.011));
   c.beginPath();
   c.ellipse(hx,hy,rr,rr*1.35,0,-Math.PI*.55,Math.PI*.55);
   c.stroke();
  }
  c.restore();
  /* Sap, running down from a split. The one warm light in a world lit gold, so it uses `enemy`
     rather than `accent` and stays on the near range. */
  if(near&&rnd()<.7){
   c.save();
   c.globalCompositeOperation='lighter';
   c.strokeStyle=pal.enemy+'44';
   c.lineWidth=Math.max(1.2,h*.01);
   const sx=cxT+(rnd()-.5)*tw*.8;
   c.beginPath();
   c.moveTo(sx,h*(.1+rnd()*.3));
   c.quadraticCurveTo(sx+h*.02,h*.6,sx-h*.01,h*.95);
   c.stroke();
   c.restore();
  }
 }
};

V26_SKYLINES.loam=function(c,w,h,pal,rnd,near){
 /* Upside down, and it is the only one. Roots come DOWN from the top of the strip, which puts the
    dark mass where every other world puts sky and is why this world feels like being underneath
    something even before the caps below are read. */
 c.fillStyle=near?'#0a0518':'#140a26';
 c.beginPath();
 c.moveTo(-10,-2);
 let x=-10;
 while(x<w+10){
  const step=h*(near?.34:.24)*(.6+rnd()*.9);
  const drop=h*(near?.5:.34)*(.3+rnd()*.9);
  c.quadraticCurveTo(x+step*.5,drop,x+step,drop*.35);
  x+=step;
 }
 c.lineTo(w+10,-2);
 c.closePath();
 c.fill();

 /* Individual roots hanging past the mass, tapering to a point. Drawn as strokes with a decreasing
    width rather than as filled triangles, because a root that bends has to keep its thickness
    through the bend. */
 x=-6;
 while(x<w+6){
  const len=h*(near?.78:.55)*(.4+rnd()*.8);
  const sway=(rnd()-.5)*h*.2;
  const top=h*(near?.2:.14)*rnd();
  c.strokeStyle=near?'#0a0518':'#140a26';
  for(const [k,wid] of [[1,.026],[.66,.016],[.33,.008]]){
   c.lineWidth=Math.max(1,h*(near?wid:wid*.66));
   c.lineCap='round';
   c.beginPath();
   c.moveTo(x,top);
   c.quadraticCurveTo(x+sway*.4,top+len*k*.6,x+sway*k,top+len*k);
   c.stroke();
  }
  x+=h*(near?.12:.08)*(.5+rnd());
 }

 /* Fungal caps on the floor, the only light source down here. Domes rather than discs, and lit at
    the crown only, so they sit on the ground instead of floating. */
 x=-4;
 while(x<w+4){
  const cw=h*(near?.09:.06)*(.5+rnd()*1.1);
  const ch=cw*(.5+rnd()*.35);
  c.save();
  c.globalCompositeOperation='lighter';
  c.fillStyle=pal.accent+(near?'44':'26');
  c.beginPath();
  c.ellipse(x,h,cw,ch,0,Math.PI,Math.PI*2);
  c.fill();
  c.fillStyle=pal.enemy+(near?'55':'2e');
  c.beginPath();
  c.ellipse(x,h-ch*.45,cw*.34,ch*.3,0,Math.PI,Math.PI*2);
  c.fill();
  c.restore();
  x+=h*(near?.2:.13)*(.5+rnd());
 }
};

V26_SKYLINES.canopy=function(c,w,h,pal,rnd,near){
 /* First version grew leaves up off stems standing on the floor and photographed as tall ferns — a
    handsome skyline and the wrong one twice over: it is not what a canopy is, and it was built from
    the same primitive as SEEDBED's sprouts, so two of six worlds shared a silhouette.

    Hung from above instead, like DEEP LOAM's roots and for the same reason: this is a world you are
    underneath. What separates it from loam is mass — broad overlapping lobes with holes between them
    rather than thin strands — and the holes are the actual subject, because what a canopy does to a
    place is let light through in patches. */
 const ink=near?'#02120a':'#062018';

 /* Shafts first, so the leaves drawn over them do the occluding. Placing dapple by hand means
    guessing where the gaps ended up; letting the canopy cast it means the light is in the gaps by
    construction. */
 c.save();
 c.globalCompositeOperation='lighter';
 for(let i=0;i<(near?5:7);i++){
  const sx=rnd()*w;
  const spread=h*(near?.3:.2);
  const g=c.createLinearGradient(sx,0,sx+spread*.6,h);
  g.addColorStop(0,pal.accent+(near?'2e':'1c'));
  g.addColorStop(1,'transparent');
  c.fillStyle=g;
  c.beginPath();
  c.moveTo(sx-spread*.1,0);
  c.lineTo(sx+spread*.1,0);
  c.lineTo(sx+spread*.75,h);
  c.lineTo(sx+spread*.25,h);
  c.closePath();c.fill();
 }
 c.restore();

 /* The mass along the top: one continuous edge, so the lobes below read as hanging off something
    rather than as free-floating leaves. */
 c.fillStyle=ink;
 c.beginPath();
 c.moveTo(-10,-2);
 let x=-10;
 while(x<w+10){
  const step=h*(near?.44:.3)*(.6+rnd()*.8);
  const drop=h*(near?.42:.3)*(.35+rnd()*.9);
  c.quadraticCurveTo(x+step*.5,drop,x+step,drop*.4);
  x+=step;
 }
 c.lineTo(w+10,-2);
 c.closePath();c.fill();

 /* Lobes hanging off the mass, three per hanger, fanned downward. The middle one is longest, which
    is what a compound leaf does and what stops the fan reading as a hand. */
 x=-h*.2;
 while(x<w+h*.2){
  const from=h*(near?.2:.14)*rnd();
  for(const [ang,scale] of [[.95,.82],[1.4,1],[1.95,.8]]){
   c.save();
   c.translate(x,from);
   c.rotate(ang+(rnd()-.5)*.28);
   c.fillStyle=ink;
   /* Broad and short: roughly two and a half to one, against the four to one of the first pass. */
   const len=h*(near?.5:.35)*scale*(.7+rnd()*.5);
   v92Leaf(c,len,h*(near?.28:.19)*scale);
   /* A midrib, and it is doing more work than its one line suggests. Broad blunt lobes in
      silhouette read as rock as readily as they read as foliage — the same shape makes a cave
      ceiling — and a single line down the length of each one is the cue that settles it. Lighter
      than the leaf rather than darker, because a dark line on a dark shape is nothing. */
   c.strokeStyle=near?'#0a2a1a':'#0e3327';
   c.lineWidth=Math.max(.8,h*.006);
   c.beginPath();
   c.moveTo(len*.04,0);
   c.lineTo(len*.82,-h*.004);
   c.stroke();
   c.restore();
  }
  /* Spaced wider than the lobes are broad, so the light drawn underneath still has somewhere to
     come through. The first pass overlapped them into one unbroken ceiling and lost the gaps,
     which are the subject. */
  x+=h*(near?.46:.31)*(.65+rnd());
 }
};

/* ---- Installed with the map ---------------------------------------------------------------------

   Wrapped rather than folded into V87, so the map machinery keeps knowing only about casts and stage
   lists and this part owns everything about how a place looks. The direct call underneath is not
   belt-and-braces: V87 and V89 both run `v87Install` at load, which is before this file exists, so
   without it the first paint of a saved Bloom save would be map one's world. */

const v92Install=v87Install;
v87Install=function(index,announce){
 const out=v92Install(index,announce);
 v92Apply(index);
 return out;
};

v92Apply(typeof v87State==='object'&&v87State?v87State.map:0);
